This paper investigates the ground state solution to the biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} where $u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4)$, and the constants $\beta$, $\lambda$, $\mu$, $c$ are given as well as the function $f$. The function $f$ is described as $f(u)=e^u-1\leq e^{|u|}$, and the author demonstrates that this equation has a standardized ground state solution under specific conditions on the constants and functions involved.
